# JSONParser Usage

## All Values Are Stored as Strings
Internally `JSONParser` uses `std::map<std::string, std::string>`. There is no runtime type info — you must call the correct typed getter.

```cpp
// ✅ GOOD — caller knows the type
int age    = json.getInt("age");
float price = json.getFloat("price");
bool active = json.getBool("active");

// ❌ BAD — getString on a number returns the raw string "42", not an int
std::string age = json.getString("age");  // "42" — probably not what you want
```

## Check Before Accessing Optional Keys
```cpp
if (json.hasKey("email")) {
    std::string email = json.getString("email");
}
```

## Loading from Files — Prefer unique_ptr
```cpp
// ✅ GOOD — memory managed automatically
auto json = loadJSONFromFile("config.json");  // returns unique_ptr<JSONParser>
if (json) {
    int port = json->getInt("PORT");
}

// ❌ BAD — manual delete required, easy to leak
JSONParser* json = getJSONFromFile("config.json");
int port = json->getInt("PORT");
delete json;
```

## Building JSON Output
```cpp
JSONParser json;
json.setString("status", "ok");
json.setInt("code", 200);
std::string output = json.toString();  // {"status":"ok","code":200}
```

## Nested Objects and Arrays
```cpp
JSONParser nested = json.getObject("address");
std::string city = nested.getString("city");

std::vector<std::string> tags = json.getStringArray("tags");
std::vector<JSONParser> items = json.getArrayOfJSON("items");
```
